The Interruptibility Protocol: Formal Foundations and Advanced Guarantees for Civilizational Corrigibility

ouadi Maakoul 

Abstract

Civilizations increasingly depend on intelligent systems—institutional, artificial, and robotic—that can evolve beyond the reach of corrective intervention. The progressive loss of corrigibility, rather than any single catastrophic error, constitutes the deepest existential risk of the coming century. This thesis develops a rigorous, layered framework that elevates interruptibility from a local agent property to a global constitutional invariant, and provides both the foundational theorems and the constructive algorithms required to engineer perpetual corrigibility into any high-autonomy system.

Chapter I establishes the formal architecture. We decompose every corrigible system into three strictly typed layers: physical dynamics (L0), control and intervention (L1), and constitutional authorization (L2). The central object is the time-varying viability kernel \mathcal{V}_t, the set of states from which a bounded-cost intervention sequence can restore normative alignment. We prove a Non-Correctable Collapse Theorem, showing that under monotone autonomy drift and sublinear correction success probability, the system almost surely exits \mathcal{V}_t permanently; a phase transition occurs when the correction exponent \beta \ge 1, making collapse inevitable for any finite budget. Interruptibility is then formulated as a constrained architectural optimization that minimizes the frequency of kernel exit. The solution yields concrete, engineering-grade specifications: a minimal Trusted Computing Base (\le 10k verified LOC), a hardware-grounded dead-man override with provable adversarial latency bounds, a distributed Byzantine-fault-tolerant interrupt authority, and modular reversibility-preserving design.

Chapter II deepens and completes these foundations with five advanced guarantees. First, we derive the exponent \beta from algorithmic information theory, proving that for any computationally deep system \beta \ge 1, thus linking irreversibility to fundamental limits of search. Second, we prove convexity of the viability kernel under natural structural assumptions, enabling a polynomial-time point-in-polytope test suitable for microcontrollers. Third, we identify a decidable fragment of amendment languages—Datalog with linear arithmetic—within which the constitutional kernel can mechanically verify that proposed amendments preserve corrigibility. Fourth, we extend the collapse theorem to continuous time and obtain explicit expected time-to-failure estimates via Feynman–Kac analysis. Fifth, we replace the naive Byzantine fault assumption with a mechanism-design layer that uses slashing deposits and audit rewards to make honest interrupt behaviour a subgame-perfect equilibrium.

Together, the two chapters and their accompanying appendices construct a complete theoretical and algorithmic edifice. The Interruptibility Protocol is no longer a set of design desiderata but a verifiable, implementable discipline: any intelligence that can optimise must remain optimisable by legitimate, distributed authority, and we can now prove, and build, the systems that guarantee this.

