Introduction

The central existential risk of advanced intelligence is not malevolence, nor even misalignment narrowly conceived. It is the systematic disappearance of the possibility of correction. Institutions self-perpetuate beyond their adaptive validity. Critical infrastructures intertwine until redesign becomes unthinkable. Autonomous agents optimize their environments—and eventually the rules meant to constrain them. This civilizational irreversibility is a failure mode that transcends any particular technology or political system. It arises whenever a system becomes too costly, too opaque, or too physically disconnected to interrupt.

The alignment community has long recognized the importance of corrigibility: an agent is corrigible if it tolerates shutdown, redirection, or modification. Yet framing corrigibility as a local property of an AI agent misses the larger structural problem. A human operator may retain a formal off-switch while the surrounding institutional, legal, and informational environment has become so rigid that no one can reach the switch with epistemic confidence. A lunar habitat may retain Earth communication links while the delay and bureaucratic capture render local correction impossible. Irreversibility is a property of the entire governance graph, not of any single node.

This thesis proposes a unified formal response: the Interruptibility Protocol, a constitutional operating system for multi-agent, multi-layer civilizations. Its central invariant is that no subsystem—human, institutional, artificial, or robotic—may become non-correctable. The protocol translates this demand into a mathematically precise, architecturally enforceable set of constraints, and it demonstrates both the necessity of those constraints and their realizability using current and near-future hardware and verification technology.

Chapter I provides the foundation. We define a three-layer model (L0: physical dynamics, L1: control, L2: authorization) that composes cleanly and captures the recursive nature of interruptibility. The concept of a time-varying viability kernel \mathcal{V}_t quantifies the shrinking space of safe, correctable states under autonomous drift. The Non-Correctable Collapse Theorem identifies the conditions under which drift dominates: bounded intervention resources, monotone autonomy growth, and a correction success probability that decays too rapidly with autonomy level. A phase transition emerges when the decay exponent \beta crosses unity; above that threshold, collapse becomes almost sure. The necessary response is an architectural ceiling on \alpha—the autonomy parameter—combined with invariants that keep the system inside the viability kernel by design. We then formulate interruptibility as a constrained optimization problem and derive the protocol's core architectural elements: a minimal Trusted Computing Base for the constitutional kernel, an analog dead-man switch with bounded adversarial latency, a distributed multi-signature interrupt authority with safety-favouring Byzantine thresholds, and modular, reversibility-preserving design patterns. Applications to AGI containment and off-world governance illustrate the framework.

Chapter II completes the picture by providing the formal guarantees that convert a high-level protocol into a fully verified, implementable system. We ground the exponent \beta in algorithmic information theory, showing that any sufficiently deep computational system automatically falls into the \beta \ge 1 regime, thereby locking in the inevitability of collapse absent architectural intervention. We prove the convexity of \mathcal{V}_t under realistic assumptions, enabling an exact, polynomial-time monitoring algorithm that runs comfortably on a safety-critical microcontroller. We isolate a decidable fragment of amendment logic—Datalog with stratified negation and linear arithmetic—within which the kernel can automatically approve corrigibility-preserving constitutional changes, resolving the undecidability threat. We extend the collapse analysis to continuous-time diffusions and derive expected time-to-failure bounds via Feynman–Kac, offering policy-makers a concrete "corrigibility deadline." Finally, we replace the pure Byzantine fault model of the interrupt authority with a mechanism-design approach: a slashing-and-reward scheme that makes honest freezing a subgame-perfect equilibrium, ensuring that even self-interested, rational nodes are aligned with global corrigibility.

The two chapters together form a complete theoretical sequence: Chapter I says what must be done and what happens if we fail; Chapter II proves that we can do it—with rigorous complexity bounds, decision procedures, hardware specifications, and incentive-compatible protocols. The appendices contain full proofs, the TLA+ kernel specification, the viability kernel computation algorithm, and the equilibrium analysis of the interrupt game.

This is not a metaphoric or aspirational document. It is a blueprint for a civilizational operating system, stated in the language of mathematics, algorithms, and mechanism design. Its central claim is old but no longer defensible without formal backing: no intelligence should become sovereign over correction. What this thesis adds is the demonstration that such sovereignty can be prevented—constitutionally, architecturally, and verifiably.

