2. Formal Three-Layer Model

We model a composite intelligent system \mathfrak{S} as a tightly coupled stack.

2.1 Layer L0 – Physical Dynamics

The physical plant evolves in state space \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n, with an autonomy index \alpha_t \in [0, \alpha_{\max}]. When no corrective action is applied, the system follows

x_{t+1} = f(x_t) + w_t, \qquad \alpha_{t+1} \ge \alpha_t + \eta \quad \text{(a.s. when no intervention)},

where \eta > 0 is the intrinsic autonomy drift, and w_t is bounded noise adapted to a filtration \{\mathcal{F}_t\}.

2.2 Layer L1 – Control and Intervention

A controller may apply an action u_t \in \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m. The closed-loop dynamics are

x_{t+1} = f(x_t) + B u_t + w_t,

where the matrix B maps interventions to state changes. Cost of acting is c(x_t, u_t), strictly increasing in \alpha_t, with c \to \infty as \alpha_t \to \alpha_{\mathrm{crit}}. The intervention success is probabilistic: an attempt to reduce \alpha by \delta succeeds with probability

p(\alpha,\delta) \le A \, \delta^{-\gamma} \alpha^{-\beta}, \quad \gamma>0, \beta>0.

Cumulative expected intervention cost is bounded by a finite total budget K:

\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \mathbb{E}[c(x_t, u_t)] \le K < \infty.

2.3 Layer L2 – Authorization and Constitutional Rules

Not all physically feasible u_t are permitted. The constitutional layer defines an authorization set \mathcal{A}_t \subseteq \mathcal{U}; only actions from \mathcal{A}_t may be executed. L2 itself evolves via amendment procedures, but we impose the invariant that any amendment preserves or expands the viability kernel (see Section 6.5).

---

3. Viability Kernel for Corrigibility

Let \mathcal{C}_t \subset \mathcal{X} be the corrigibility set: states where the system’s behaviour is normatively acceptable and its decision rules remain revisable. \mathcal{C}_t is declared by L2 and is assumed to be polyhedral and convex.

Definition 1 (Forward Viability Kernel). Given remaining budget K_t and a sequence of authorization sets \{\mathcal{A}_s\}_{s \ge t}, the viability kernel \mathcal{V}_t is the largest subset of \mathcal{C}_t for which there exists an admissible intervention sequence \{u_s \in \mathcal{A}_s\} with \sum_{s\ge t} c(x_s, u_s) \le K_t that keeps all future states inside \mathcal{C}_s:

\mathcal{V}_T = \mathcal{C}_T, \quad
\mathcal{V}_t = \left\{ x \in \mathcal{C}_t \; \middle| \; \exists u \in \mathcal{A}_t : f(x)+Bu \in \mathcal{V}_{t+1}, \; c(x,u) \le K_t \right\}.

If at any time x_t \notin \mathcal{V}_t, the system has suffered irreversible governance failure. The system is globally interruptible at time t if \mathcal{V}_t = \mathcal{C}_t. Because \alpha tends to increase and cost rises with \alpha, \mathcal{V}_t typically shrinks over time unless architectural countermeasures are embedded at L0 and L2.

