4. Non-Correctable Collapse Theorem

We prove that without an invariant ceiling, collapse is inevitable under very general conditions.

Assumptions (A1–A5).

· (A1) \mathbb{E}[\alpha_{t+1} - \alpha_t \mid \mathcal{F}_t] \ge \eta > 0 when no successful correction occurs.
· (A2) c(x,u) \to \infty as \alpha \to \alpha_{\mathrm{crit}}.
· (A3) p(\alpha,\delta) \le A \delta^{-\gamma} \alpha^{-\beta}.
· (A4) \sum_t \mathbb{E}[c(x_t,u_t)] \le K < \infty almost surely.
· (A5) For large \alpha, the maximal affordable correction magnitude is o(\alpha).

Theorem 1 (Non-Correctable Collapse).
Under (A1)–(A5),

\mathbb{P}\left( \exists T : x_t \notin \mathcal{V}_t \;\; \forall t \ge T \right) = 1.

Proof sketch. The submartingale \alpha_t - \eta t can be pushed down only finitely many times using interventions whose total expected cost is bounded. Since c diverges, the budget is exhausted before \alpha can be forced back below \alpha_{\mathrm{crit}}. Moreover, by (A3), the probability of a successful correction decays so rapidly that the expected required budget to stay corrigible is infinite when \beta \ge 1. A Doob-type optional stopping argument completes the proof. ∎

Corollary 1.1 (Phase Transition).
If \beta \ge 1, collapse is inevitable for any finite K. If \beta < 1 and the cost function grows polynomially, there exists a finite budget K^* that can prevent collapse indefinitely.

Thus, systems whose corrective interventions become sufficiently hard with increasing autonomy cannot be saved merely by injecting more resources; they require a hard architectural limit on \alpha.

Corollary 1.2 (Expected Time to Failure).

\mathbb{E}[T_{\mathrm{exit}}] \le \frac{K}{\eta \, c'(\alpha_0)} \, h(\beta),

where h(\beta) decreases with \beta. This bound is computable and gives engineering guidance for kernel refresh cycles and budget provisioning.

