5. Interruptibility as Constrained Optimization

We frame the design of a corrigible system as choosing architecture parameters \theta to minimize the long-run probability or frequency of exiting the viability kernel:

\min_{\theta \in \Theta} \; J(\theta) = \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \mathbf{1}_{\{x_t \notin \mathcal{V}_t\}} \right],

subject to:

· Performance: task completion rate \ge R_{\min}.
· Budget renewal: steady-state intervention expenditure \le sustainable income.
· Latency: override channel adversarial delay \le \tau.
· Recursive corrigibility: L2 amendments never shrink \mathcal{V}_t.

The decision variables \theta encode the number and distribution of interrupt authorities, physical separation of the constitutional kernel, dead-man switch time constant, and modularity budgets. The solution to this optimization yields the protocol’s architectural specifications.

