6. Architectural Design and Algorithms

6.1 Minimal Trusted Computing Base (TCB)

The Constitutional Kernel is the lowest-level enforcement engine. It runs on a physically distinct microcontroller with isolated power and network. Its specification in TLA+ guarantees that the system cannot remain in “Run” state when a heartbeat from any sovereign authority is absent. The implementation is bounded to \le 10\,000 lines of verified C (no dynamic memory, no scheduling). This TCB monitors the viability kernel and triggers a freeze on violation.

6.2 Hardware Dead-Man Switch with Adversarial Latency Bounds

Human override is not a software button; it is an analog circuit. A capacitor is continuously recharged by cryptographically signed “no-objection” tokens from an air-gapped Hardware Security Module (HSM). A human must physically interact with the HSM to produce a token. If valid tokens stop arriving for longer than time constant \tau, the capacitor discharges and a relay physically disconnects the actuators. An attacker who compromises L1 or L2 cannot extend \tau without physical access to the HSM. This gives the guaranteed adversarial latency bound demanded by the optimization.

6.3 Distributed Interrupt Authority as Byzantine Protocol

We require N = 3f+1 sovereign interrupt nodes. The freeze-unfreeze protocol uses asymmetric thresholds:

· Freeze: any single node can broadcast a freeze; the interlock trips upon receipt at any correct node.
· Unfreeze: requires a BLS threshold signature from \ge 2f+1 distinct nodes.

Thus compromising \le f nodes cannot prevent a legitimate freeze (safety) nor produce a spurious unfreeze (liveness for safety). The system favours safety over uninterrupted operation.

6.4 Real-Time Viability Kernel Check

Because \mathcal{V}_t lies in a low-dimensional space (autonomy, budget, legitimacy), it can be precomputed offline as a convex polytope using dynamic programming, then represented as a set of linear inequalities H^* x \le b^*. The kernel performs an O(d) point-in-polytope test at runtime (d \le 5). On a Cortex-M7 microcontroller, this takes less than 20 µs, well within cycle budgets.

Algorithm 1 (Kernel Monitoring Loop).

```
loop every Δ ms:
    read sensors → x = (α, K, legitimacy)
    if not (H^* x <= b^*).all():
        trigger freeze
    else:
        continue
```

6.5 Recursive Corrigibility via Amendment Lattice

L2 amendments are filtered. We define the set of amendments that preserve or expand \mathcal{V}_t as a meet-semilattice. An amendment is accepted only if the new viability polytope contains the old one: H_{\mathrm{new}} x \le b_{\mathrm{new}} \supseteq H_{\mathrm{old}} x \le b_{\mathrm{old}}. For small dimensions, this containment is verified by solving a linear programming feasibility problem. This syntactic check (Farkas’ lemma) guarantees corrigibility preservation for a rich class of constitutional rules.

