2. Correction Search Complexity and the Inevitability of Collapse (Proposal 1)

2.1 Micro-Foundations of p(\alpha,\delta)

The earlier collapse theorem treated the success probability p(\alpha,\delta) of an intervention of size \delta when autonomy is \alpha as an exogenously given function. Here we derive its asymptotic form from the computational difficulty of locating a valid correction in a system whose internal state has high Kolmogorov complexity.

Let the system’s L0 state S_t be described by a binary string of length L(S_t). The autonomy index \alpha_t is defined as the Kolmogorov complexity K(S_t)—the length of the shortest program that outputs S_t on a universal Turing machine. As the system self-optimizes, its internal regularities become more efficient, and its shortest description length grows (this corresponds to the intuition that deeply optimized systems are algorithmically random in appearance). A correction is a program \pi that, given S_t, outputs a modified state S_{t+1} with strictly lower \alpha. The size of the intervention |\pi| we denote by \delta.

Assumption 1 (Computational Depth). The system’s mapping from current autonomy to the space of possible corrective programs is computationally deep: any program that reduces \alpha by at least 1 must exploit a regularity of complexity at least \alpha, and the number of such effectively exploitable regularities is bounded by O(\alpha).

This is a mild assumption for any system that results from long optimization processes, and it is consistent with Bennett’s concept of logical depth: deeply organized structures resist efficient modification by short programs.

Lemma 2.1 (Success Probability Upper Bound). Under Assumption 1, for any \delta, the fraction of all programs of length \le \delta that successfully reduce \alpha by at least 1 is bounded above by O(\alpha^{-1}). More precisely, there exists a constant A such that for all sufficiently large \alpha,

p(\alpha,\delta) \le A \, \alpha^{-1} \, 2^{\delta}.

Proof. The total number of programs of length \le \delta is N = 2^{\delta+1}-1 = O(2^\delta). Among these, a valid correction must incorporate at least one of the O(\alpha) regularities that discriminate a lower-\alpha configuration. By a counting argument, at most O(\alpha \cdot 2^{\delta - \alpha}) programs can encode one such regularity because specifying a regularity requires approximately \alpha bits. For \alpha large compared to \delta, most such programs are impossible; the fraction is bounded by O(\alpha \cdot 2^{-\alpha}) \le O(\alpha^{-1}) (since 2^{-\alpha} decays faster). A tighter bound via Levin’s universal search yields the same asymptotic. ∎

Corollary 2.1. If we model the success probability for intervention magnitude \delta as p(\alpha,\delta) = \Theta(\delta^{-\gamma} \alpha^{-\beta}) as in Chapter I, then necessarily \beta \ge 1 for any computationally deep system.

Proof. From Lemma 2.1, the \alpha-dependence is at most order \alpha^{-1}, regardless of \delta, implying \beta \ge 1. ∎

Theorem 2.2 (Inevitability of Collapse for Deep Systems). For any civilizational system whose L0 dynamics satisfy computational depth, the exponent in the collapse theorem satisfies \beta \ge 1. Consequently, by Corollary 1.1 of Chapter I, collapse is inevitable for any finite intervention budget K.

This derivation bridges the abstract notion of “autonomy” to the fundamental limits of computation: highly optimized systems become algorithmically opaque, and correcting them becomes exponentially harder as they grow more complex. The only escape is to impose a hard architectural ceiling on \alpha, i.e., to prevent the system from entering regimes of high Kolmogorov complexity.

2.2 Estimation Algorithm for \beta

While the theoretical bound guarantees \beta \ge 1 for deep systems, in practice one may measure the effective \beta from simulation or historical data to calibrate the risk horizon.

Algorithm 1 (Empirical Estimation of \beta).
Given a historical dataset of interventions ( \alpha_i, \delta_i, \text{outcome}_i ), fit a logistic regression: \log\frac{p}{1-p} = \gamma \log\delta + \beta \log\alpha + c. Return \hat{\beta}. If \hat{\beta} < 1, the system has not yet entered the deep regime or exhibits exploitable structural simplifications.

