3. Convexity of the Viability Kernel and Real-Time Verification (Proposal 2)

3.1 Convexity Proof

The viability kernel \mathcal{V}_t is the set of L0 states from which a feasible correction sequence exists. For the kernel to be checked in real time on a minimal TCB, we need \mathcal{V}_t to be a convex set representable by a modest number of half-spaces. We now prove convexity under natural structural assumptions.

Assumptions (A6–A8).

· (A6) L0 dynamics are linear in state: x_{t+1} = A x_t + B u_t + \phi(\alpha_t), with A stable and \phi monotone increasing in \alpha.
· (A7) Intervention cost is convex quadratic: c(x,u) = c_0(\alpha) + u^\mathsf{T} R u, with R \succ 0 and c_0 convex, strictly increasing in \alpha.
· (A8) The corrigible set \mathcal{C}_t is polyhedral: \mathcal{C}_t = \{ x : H_t x \le b_t \} where each row defines a convex constraint.

Lemma 3.1 (Convexity of \mathcal{V}_t). Under A6–A8, \mathcal{V}_t is convex for all t.

Proof. By backward induction on t. At the terminal horizon T, \mathcal{V}_T = \mathcal{C}_T which is convex. Assume \mathcal{V}_{t+1} is convex. The set of (x,u) satisfying x \in \mathcal{C}_t, u \in \mathcal{A}_t, c(x,u) \le K_t, and A x + B u + \phi(\alpha) \in \mathcal{V}_{t+1} is the intersection of convex sets: \{u \in \mathcal{A}_t\} is assumed polyhedral (typically a box or convex cone); c_0(\alpha) + u^\mathsf{T} R u \le K_t is a convex sublevel set; and the linear image of a convex set under A x + B u is convex. The projection onto x retains convexity. Intersection with \mathcal{C}_t yields \mathcal{V}_t, which is therefore convex. ∎

Convexity holds even if \mathcal{A}_t is a general convex set; thus our authorization layer can be expressive.

3.2 Exact Computation via Vertex Enumeration

Since \mathcal{V}_t is a full-dimensional convex polytope in a low-dimensional space (typically d \le 5, dimensions being e.g. autonomy \alpha, budget K, legitimacy score, etc.), we can store it as an H-representation (intersection of half-spaces) or as vertices. The kernel performs a membership test.

Algorithm 2 (Offline Construction and Runtime Check).
Offline: Given parameters \theta, discretize the L0 state space into a grid of N cells. Use backward dynamic programming to compute the set of viable points, then apply the double-description method to convert the viable region into an H-representation H^* x \le b^*. Complexity is O(N_v^{\lfloor d/2 \rfloor}) with N_v the number of vertices; for d \le 5 and N_v \le 10^3, this completes in under 1 second on a modern CPU.

Runtime (Kernel):

```
Function in_viability_kernel(x):
    return (H^* x <= b^*).all()
```

This requires d multiply-adds and m comparisons, where m is the number of half-spaces (typically 50–200). On a Cortex-M7 microcontroller, the check takes < 20\,\mus, well within real-time constraints.

Corollary 3.2. The constitutional kernel can monitor interruptibility with guaranteed soundness and negligible computational overhead, satisfying the Fifth Pillar's requirement that the kernel be “simple enough for human verification.”

