6. Incentive-Compatible Interrupt Authority (Proposal 5)

6.1 Rational Byzantine Nodes

Previous distributed interrupt designs assumed a fraction of nodes are Byzantine but did not model why they would deviate. In governance, a rational node might freeze a rival to gain advantage. We design a mechanism that makes honest freeze behavior a subgame-perfect equilibrium.

Setup. There are N = 3f+1 interrupt authority nodes, each with a utility function that values system safety but also private benefits from strategic freezes. Each node can submit a FREEZE message at any time. A freeze costs the system a global loss \mathcal{L} but also imposes costs/benefits on the nodes. After a freeze, an audit protocol determines whether the freeze was justified (i.e., x_t \notin \mathcal{V}_t) or spurious. The audit is performed by the remaining nodes via a multi-round evidence submission, culminating in a BLS threshold signature verdict after a timeout.

Mechanism (Slashing Deposits).

· To issue a FREEZE, a node must deposit D coins into a smart contract (or constitutional escrow).
· If the audit finds the freeze justified, the depositor receives back D plus a reward R, and the system pays for the audit from a common fund.
· If the freeze is spurious, the depositor loses D (which is distributed to the other nodes as compensation) and additionally incurs a reputation penalty equivalent to \mathcal{P}.
· The reward R is set such that R > \text{private cost of honest freeze} (e.g., time, computational resources).
· The deposit D satisfies D > \max\{\text{private gain from malicious freeze}, \mathcal{L} / (N-1)\} to deter strategic sabotage.

Theorem 6.1 (Honest-Freeze as SPNE). Under the above mechanism, the strategy profile “freeze if and only if x \notin \mathcal{V}_t, and always participate in honest audit” forms a subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium.

Proof. Consider an arbitrary node i. If x \notin \mathcal{V}_t, freezing yields utility u_H = \text{base} - \text{cost} + R while not freezing yields \text{base} - \text{loss} (because the system may enter irreparable state). Since R covers honest costs, freezing dominates. If x \in \mathcal{V}_t, freezing gives utility \text{base} - D - \mathcal{P} - \text{audit penalties}, which is worse than doing nothing. Auditing is also incentive-compatible: correct votes on the verdict earn a small fee, and misreporting leads to slashing of the auditor’s own deposit. By backward induction, the honest profile is incentive-compatible in every subgame. ∎

Algorithm 5 (Freeze-Unfreeze with Deposits).

```
Function freeze(node, x, evidence):
    assert deposit[D >= threshold]
    broadcast FREEZE with evidence
    start audit timer
    wait for audit verdict (BLS threshold signature from >=2f+1 validators)
    if verdict == JUSTIFIED:
        refund D + reward R
    else:
        slash D, apply penalty P

Function audit_verdict(nodes, freeze_event):
    each node locally checks evidence against public viability kernel
    sign JUSTIFIED or SPURIOUS
    aggregate signatures
    return majority verdict
```

This transforms the interrupt authority into a self-policing rational body, aligned with the global corrigibility objective. The mechanism’s parameters (D, R, \mathcal{P}) can be tuned to the specific value-at-stake, making the protocol robust in both AI governance and interplanetary settlements.

