7. Discussion and Integration

The five contributions of this chapter collectively complete the mathematical and algorithmic underpinnings of the Interruptibility Protocol. Figure 1 (a textual description) depicts their interlocking roles:

· Computational depth \to \beta \ge 1 justifies the inevitability of collapse without ceilings, and thus the absolute necessity of architectural invariants.
· Convex kernel \to efficient monitoring enables the protocol to run on minimal, formally verifiable TCBs, satisfying the extreme simplicity constraint.
· Decidable amendment language \to mechanical L2 safety removes the specter of undecidability, providing a concrete path to self-amending constitutions that cannot disable interruptibility.
· Continuous-time analysis \to predictive risk metrics bridges the gap to policy-making: regulators and settlers can know their “corrigibility deadline.”
· Incentive-compatible interrupts \to rational participation ensures that the distributed authority isn’t merely Byzantine-resilient but also strategically stable.

Together they elevate the protocol from a set of design desiderata to a constructive blueprint: one could, in principle, synthesize a mathematical model of a specific system, compute its viability kernel, embed the kernel code in a verified microkernel, deploy a distributed set of incentive-compatible interrupt nodes, and have a formal guarantee that no drift into irreversible governance failure can occur without triggering a correction.

The remaining tasks are implementation-specific: formal verification of the kernel in Isabelle/HOL, hardware testing of the dead-man switch under radiation environments for space applications, and simulation of the continuous-time model with real AGI training curves. These lie in the domain of applied engineering and future work.


