Appendix A

Proof of the Non-Correctable Collapse Theorem (Chapter I, Section 4)

We restate the theorem with complete notation.

A.1 Model and Assumptions

Let (\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \{\mathcal{F}_t\}_{t \ge 0}, \mathbb{P}) be a filtered probability space. The autonomy process \alpha_t is adapted, takes values in [0, \alpha_{\max}] (where \alpha_{\max} may be infinite). The cost function c : [0, \alpha_{\max}] \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} is strictly increasing, c(0)=0, and \lim_{\alpha \to \alpha_{\rm crit}} c(\alpha) = \infty for some \alpha_{\rm crit} \le \alpha_{\max}.

An intervention at time t consists of an action u_t \in \mathcal{U} with magnitude \delta_t = \|u_t\|. Success is modelled by a random variable S_t \in \{0,1\} where \mathbb{P}(S_t = 1 \mid \mathcal{F}_t, u_t) \le p(\alpha_t, \delta_t), with p satisfying

p(\alpha,\delta) \le A \delta^{-\gamma} \alpha^{-\beta} \qquad (A>0,\ \gamma>0,\ \beta>0).

If S_t=1, \alpha is reduced by \delta_t; otherwise it remains unchanged (or increases minimally). For simplicity, we treat \alpha_t as a real-valued process and model the effect of a successful intervention as an instantaneous reduction of \delta_t; the precise jump dynamics do not affect the asymptotic result.

The cumulative expected cost of all interventions is bounded:

\mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} c(\alpha_t) \cdot \mathbf{1}_{\{u_t \neq 0\}} \right] \le K < \infty.

Without intervention, \alpha_t drifts upward:

\mathbb{E}[\alpha_{t+1} - \alpha_t \mid \mathcal{F}_t, u_t=0] \ge \eta > 0.

If a failed intervention occurs, the drift still applies.

A.2 Lemma: Finite Expected Number of Successful Corrections

Let N be the total number of successful interventions that achieve \delta_t \ge 1. Because each successful intervention costs at least c(\alpha_t) \ge c(0+\epsilon) and cost is increasing, and the total expected budget is K, the expected number of successful interventions cannot exceed K / \min_{0 \le \alpha \le \alpha_{\rm crit}} c(\alpha) = M < \infty. Thus \mathbb{E}[N] \le M.

A.3 Submartingale Argument

Define the process Y_t = \alpha_t - \eta t. In the absence of intervention, \mathbb{E}[Y_{t+1} - Y_t \mid \mathcal{F}_t] \ge 0. A successful intervention reduces \alpha by at least 1 (if we standardize \delta_t \ge 1 for effective corrections). Thus Y_t is a submartingale except at times of successful correction, where it can jump downward. However, since the number of such downward jumps has finite expectation, the unconditional expectation \mathbb{E}[Y_t] \to \infty as t \to \infty. Consequently, \alpha_t must eventually exceed any finite level \alpha_{\rm crit}. Once \alpha_t > \alpha_{\rm crit}, the cost of any further intervention exceeds the remaining budget, so no further correction is possible. Moreover, because the success probability decays as \alpha^{-\beta} with \beta \ge 1, even if the budget permitted an attempt, the probability that it would succeed before hitting the budget constraint goes to zero. Formalising this via a Borel-Cantelli argument yields almost sure permanent exit from the viability kernel.

A.4 Phase Transition

For \beta < 1, one can design an intervention policy that, with finite budget, keeps \alpha below a threshold forever with positive probability. The proof follows by constructing a renewal-like process: when \alpha approaches the threshold, spend a lump sum to push it back by an amount that succeeds with probability \sim \alpha^{-\beta}; the expected cost per unit of reduction is proportional to \alpha^{\beta-1}, which is integrable if \beta<1. Hence a finite budget suffices to maintain a steady-state.

