Appendix B

Convexity of the Viability Kernel (Chapter II, Section 3)

We work under Assumptions (A6)–(A8) of the main text.

B.1 Definitions

Let \mathcal{V}_t be defined recursively as:

\mathcal{V}_T = \mathcal{C}_T, \qquad
\mathcal{V}_t = \left\{ x \in \mathcal{C}_t \mid \exists u \in \mathcal{A}_t : A x + B u + \phi(\alpha(x)) \in \mathcal{V}_{t+1},\ c(x,u) \le K_t \right\}.

Here \alpha(x) is a linear projection onto the autonomy coordinate (since state includes \alpha as a component). The cost is c(x,u) = c_0(\alpha(x)) + u^{\mathsf{T}} R u with R \succ 0 and c_0 convex increasing. \mathcal{C}_t = \{x : H_t x \le b_t\} is a convex polyhedron; \mathcal{A}_t is a convex set.

B.2 Induction proof

Base case t=T: \mathcal{V}_T = \mathcal{C}_T is convex.

Inductive step: Assume \mathcal{V}_{t+1} is convex. Consider the set

\mathcal{Q}_t = \{ (x,u) \mid x \in \mathcal{C}_t,\ u \in \mathcal{A}_t,\ A x + B u + \phi(\alpha(x)) \in \mathcal{V}_{t+1},\ c(x,u) \le K_t \}.

· \mathcal{C}_t is convex; \mathcal{A}_t is convex.
· The condition A x + B u + \phi(\alpha(x)) \in \mathcal{V}_{t+1} is an intersection of half-space constraints of the form H'(A x + B u + \phi) \le b' (since \mathcal{V}_{t+1} is a polyhedron). This defines a convex set in (x,u) because each such inequality is affine in x,u (note that \phi(\alpha(x)) is linear in x as \alpha(x) is linear).
· The inequality c_0(\alpha(x)) + u^{\mathsf{T}} R u \le K_t: since c_0 is convex and \alpha(x) is linear, c_0(\alpha(x)) is convex in x; u^{\mathsf{T}} R u is convex; thus the sublevel set is convex.

Thus \mathcal{Q}_t is the intersection of convex sets, hence convex. Then \mathcal{V}_t = \operatorname{proj}_x(\mathcal{Q}_t) is convex because projection preserves convexity, and since we intersect with \mathcal{C}_t (already done), the result follows.

