Appendix E

Incentive-Compatible Interrupt Mechanism: Formal Game and Equilibrium (Chapter II, Section 6)

E.1 Game Description

We model the interrupt decision as an extensive-form game with N = 3f+1 players, each indexed by i. There are two stages: (1) a move phase where each player can choose to send a FREEZE message or do nothing; (2) an audit phase where players vote on whether the freeze was justified. The state of the system (in viability kernel or not) is public but not contractible (cannot be enforced by smart contract directly); instead, an audit protocol with majority vote determines the outcome.

Utilities:

· If system is safe and no freeze: all get normalized payoff 0.
· If system is unsafe (outside kernel) and no freeze: all get -L (catastrophic loss).
· If a freeze occurs, the system enters a frozen state. After audit:
  · If freeze is justified (true unsafe state), the frozen system avoids loss -L. The originator receives reward R; others receive 0.
  · If freeze is spurious (safe state frozen), the originator loses deposit D and incurs penalty \mathcal{P}; the frozen system causes a smaller inconvenience -l to everyone, but originator’s net payoff is -D-\mathcal{P}-l, while others receive -l plus a share of the slashed deposit.
· Voting in the audit: voting honestly yields a small fee f; voting dishonestly (contradicting the true state) results in a slash of the voter’s deposit if detected.

E.2 Equilibrium Conditions

We impose the following conditions on the parameters:

1. R > \text{cost of honest freeze} (e.g., computation cost).
2. D > \max\{ \text{private gain from spurious freeze}, \frac{L}{N-1} \}.
3. \mathcal{P} large enough to deter.
4. Audit slashing threats make truthful reporting a dominant strategy.

Under these, the strategy profile “freeze only when system is outside viability kernel, and always vote truthfully in audit” forms a subgame-perfect Nash equilibrium. The proof uses backward induction: in the audit subgame, truth-telling dominates; given this, a player contemplating a spurious freeze knows she will lose D+\mathcal{P}+l which is worse than not freezing. An honest freeze when unsafe yields R > \text{cost}, better than the catastrophic loss -L from not freezing. Thus the profile is sequentially rational.

