Appendix G

Offline Viability Kernel Computation Algorithm (Chapter I, Section 6.4)

G.1 Problem

Compute the set \mathcal{V}_0 given discrete-time dynamics, cost function, budget horizon T. We assume the state space has been appropriately discretised on a grid G \subset \mathbb{R}^d with N points.

G.2 Dynamic Programming

```plaintext
Algorithm ComputeKernel(T, K_total, dynamics, cost_func, C_t):
    // Terminal condition
    for each x in G:
        if x in C_T: V[x][T] = True, else V[x][T] = False

    // Backward induction
    for t = T-1 downto 0:
        for each x in G:
            V[x][t] = False
            if x in C_t:
                for each u in allowed_actions(x, t) with cost <= K_remaining[t]:
                    x_next = dynamics(x, u)
                    if x_next in G and V[x_next][t+1]:
                        V[x][t] = True
                        break
        // propagate K_remaining implicitly via budget partition; simplified here.

    // Viability kernel at t=0 is set of x with V[x][0] = True
    return { x | V[x][0] }
```

The budget constraint is handled by a multi-dimensional state including remaining budget, which increases grid size but for small dimensions remains manageable.

G.3 Polytope Conversion

Once the viable set is obtained as a point cloud, we compute its convex hull (using QuickHull) if convexity is proven (Appendix B), obtaining a polytope in half-space representation. The double-description method can switch between V- and H-representations with complexity O(N_v^{\lfloor d/2 \rfloor}). For d=5 and N_v \le 10^3, this takes milliseconds on modern hardware.

The final H-representation H^* x \le b^* is embedded into the kernel's flash memory.

